.TE
.SS See-through windows
.TP
.BI "opacity = " number ", opacity_unfocused = " number
From 0.05 to 1: the focused window's opacity, and every other one's.
A fullscreen window is always solid.
Rules can set their own with
.B opacity
and
.BR opacity_unfocused .
Default: 1.
.TP
.BR window_background " = auto | \fIcolour\fP"
What shows behind an app's see-through parts, such as a terminal with a
translucent background.
.B auto
is the frame colour;
.B 00000000
shows what is behind the window.
Default: auto.
.TP
.BI "header_gloss = " n
A glossy highlight across window headers, as on Windows 7's Aero glass:
brighter above the middle, with a hard edge there.
0 to 1; 0 is a flat header.
Default: 0.
.TP
.BI "header_opacity = " n
How solid window headers are, 0.05 to 1.
Below 1, what is behind the window shows through its header, and with
.B blur
on it is blurred like frosted glass.
Needs a SceneFX build; elsewhere headers stay solid.
Default: 1.
.TP
.BI "blur = " bool
Blur what shows through see-through windows.
Needs a SceneFX build, which the Vulkan renderer (and so
.BR hdr )
is not, and costs GPU time on every frame.
Default: false.
.TP
.BI "blur_passes = " n ", blur_radius = " n
How smooth and how wide the blur is, 1 to 10 and 1 to 100; the overview's
blur uses them too.
Default: 2 and 5.
.TP
.BI "blur_layers = " "namespace ..."
Layer surfaces to blur behind, by namespace, as globs: bars, launchers,
notifications.
.B aroctl
does not list namespaces; the log does, as each surface maps.
.SH MOTION
.TS
tab(|);
lb lb lb
l l l.
key|default|meaning
anim_ms|340|window movement, with spring overshoot
fullscreen_anim_ms|220|into and out of fullscreen
focus_anim_ms|160|
drop_anim_ms|160|
workspace_slide_ms|220|0 is the same as workspace_slide = off
open_scale|0.88|starting size of a new window
overview_zoom|0.5|
overview_ms|250|
switcher_delay_ms|150|a quicker tap swaps without showing the switcher
switcher_debounce_ms|750|how long focus must stay to count as used
switcher_preview_height|260|
notify_ms|6000|how long messages stay
notify_max_width|520|
drag_threshold|12|pixels before a dragged tiled window tears out
resize_zone|8|border grab area, in pixels
resize_step|0.04|resize per key press, as a share
.TE
.SH STARTUP
.TP
.BI "exec = " command
Run once when aro starts, with
.BR /bin/sh .
.TP
.BI "exec_always = " command
Run at start and again on every reload; earlier copies are not stopped.
.SH RULES
.B rule =
.IR match ...
.IR action ...
.PP
A match is
.BI app_id: pattern\fR,
.BI title: pattern
or
.BI type: pattern\fR;
patterns are case-insensitive globs with
.B *
and
.BR ? ,
quoted when they contain spaces.
Every match on a line must hold.
.B app_id
is WM_CLASS for X11 windows;
.B type
is normal, dialog, splash, utility, toolbar, menu and so on.
Dialogs, utilities, toolbars and splash screens float without a rule.
.PP
An action is
.BR float ,
.BR tile ,
.BR fullscreen ,
.BI workspace " n"\fR,
.B scratchpad
(opens the window hidden in the scratchpad),
.B sticky
(floating, on every workspace),
.BI monitor " name"
(opens it on the screen whose connector name or
.RI \(dq make " " model " " serial \(dq
matches the glob, as monitor blocks do),
.BI size " w h"
and
.BI position " x y"
(a floating window's size, frame included, and top-left corner in the
screen's usable area; each in pixels, or a share of the screen like
.BR 60% ),
.B center
(floating, centred),
or
.BR no_border ,
.B no_header
and
.BR no_radius ,
which take that part of the window's frame away,
or
.BI opacity " n"
and
.BI opacity_unfocused " n"\fR.
Rules apply top to bottom, the last one winning, whenever a window opens,
changes title or app_id, or the config is reloaded.
.PP
.nf
rule = app_id:mpv  workspace 3
rule = app_id:firefox  title:Picture-in-Picture  float
rule = type:dialog  app_id:gimp*  tile
rule = app_id:dropterm  scratchpad
rule = app_id:pavucontrol  float  size 800 500  center
rule = app_id:mpv  no_border  no_header  no_radius
.fi
.SH BINDINGS
.B bind =
.IB combo ", " action\fR[\fB, \fIargument\fR]
.PP
A combo is modifiers and a key joined by
.BR + :
.BR mod ,
.BR super " (or " logo ),
.BR alt ,
.BR ctrl " (or " control ),
.BR shift ,
and an XKB key name such as
.BR Return ,
.B Tab
or
.BR h ,
or a mouse button:
.BR mouse_left ,
.BR mouse_right ,
.BR mouse_middle ,
.B mouse_back
or
.BR mouse_forward .
A bound mouse button runs its action and the app under the pointer sees
neither the press nor the release; such bindings take precedence over
.B mod+drag
and
.BR mod+right-drag .
The first
.B bind
line replaces all default bindings; see
.BR aro (1).
.TP
.BI spawn " command"
Run a command.
.B exec
is the same.
.TP
.BR close ", " quit ", " float ", " fullscreen ", " overview
.TP
.BR focus ", " move ", " resize " " left | right | up | down
Across monitors too, by the same rule.
.TP
.B maximize
On a
.B scroll
workspace, widen the focused column to the whole screen; again, and it
goes back to the width it had.
.TP
.BR width " [" next | prev ]
On a
.B scroll
workspace, give the focused column the next wider
.RB ( prev :
narrower) width from
.BR scroll_presets ,
wrapping round at the end.
.TP
.B sticky
Keep the focused window on every workspace of its monitor, floating; a
tiled window floats first.
Tiling it again, or sending it to a workspace, ends it.
.TP
.B scratchpad
Hide the focused window in the scratchpad, floating; on a scratchpad window
that is showing, take it back out, leaving it where it is.
.TP
.BR scratchpad_show " [\fIapp_id\fP]"
Bring the most recently used scratchpad window, or the most recent whose
app_id matches the glob, to the current workspace and focus it; if it is
already showing there, hide it again.
Clicking a hidden one in a taskbar brings it out too.
.TP
.BR group " " left | right | up | down
Put the focused window into the tile that way as a tab.
A tile of tabs shows one window at a time, with the others' titles as tabs
in its header; it moves, resizes and swaps as one.
Dragging a window by its title onto another's title does the same.
.TP
.B ungroup
Take the focused window out of its tabs, into a tile of its own beside them.
.TP
.BR tab " [" next | prev ]
Show the next or previous tab.
Clicking a tab, or turning the wheel over the tabs, does too.
.TP
.B wallpapers
Open the wallpaper picker on the focused monitor, or close it.
Each wallpaper is a card cut to the screen's shape, the way it will cover
it.
Left and right, Tab, Page Up and Down, Home and End, the wheel, or the
.BR focus " left and right binds move through them;"
typing searches by name, Backspace takes a character back and Escape
clears the search, then closes.
Enter, or clicking the middle card, shows it as the wallpaper and closes;
clicking another card moves to it; Ctrl+R jumps to a random one.
The choice is remembered like
.BR "aroctl wallpaper" 's.
.TP
.BR move_workspace " " left | right | up | down
Move the current workspace, with its windows and layout, to the monitor in
that direction: to the same number there if it is free, otherwise to the
first empty one.
.TP
.BI workspace " n"\fR,\fP " sendto" " n"
Go to, or send the window to, workspace 1 to 32.
.TP
.BR split " " right | down
Where the next window opens.
.TP
.BR switch " " next | prev
The window switcher.
.TP
.BR layout " " toggle | manual | dwindle | monocle
.B toggle
cycles the three.
.PP
The same actions work from the command line, as
.BR "aroctl dispatch" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/aro/config.example
An annotated example with every setting.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR aro (1),
.BR aroctl (1),
.BR aropaper (1)
